A||||10||Dan010||Idle when a/c is turned on||||1||
Z||000000||Dan010||10-26-2001||08:14 AM||||When a/c is turned on, what changes the Idle for the extra load, I know the a/c switch tells the ecm that it is on, but what is used to increase idle, my 86 2.8 idle does not seem to increase, may decrease some what.<BR>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Rodrv6||10-26-2001||08:46 AM||||The ECM opens up the idle air controller a little to make up for the additional load caused by the A/C compresser.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dan010||10-26-2001||09:56 AM||||Could it be that the iac is not working properly since I do not see any changes to idle speed when I turn on the a/c?<BR>Is there any other sensors that would effect<BR>this?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||10-26-2001||11:02 AM||||How is the charge on the A/C?<BR>If you don't have enough charge, the A/C clutch will not engauge and the Idle will remain the same.<BR>Something to look at.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Dan010||10-26-2001||11:53 AM||||Charge seem to be ok, clutch works, cycles<BR>on and of, cold air ect. just dont seem to have increase in rpm when a/c is turned on.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Dan010||10-26-2001||04:02 PM||||Any more thoughts?||||reg||1
Z||000006||kiteman||10-27-2001||08:12 AM||||I seem to remember in the archives a thread that talked about two types of alternators - one for engines with A/C and one for engines without A/C.  The discussion talked about the non-A/C alternator in cars with A/C giving lower and rough idle, since it put out less amps then was needed at idle with the A/C on.  I think it also talked about how you can tell the difference by looking at the alternator.  Might be worth a shot to look there.<P>If you try it, let us know how it turns out.||||reg||3
Z||000007||Dan010||10-27-2001||07:37 PM||||Thanks, I shall thake a look||||reg||1
Z||000008||robertjay||10-28-2001||02:44 AM||||If I'm not mistaken, the ECU doesn't try to up the idle when you turn the a/c on, it just tries to keep a set idle rpm.  Like if you were sitting still and you let the clutch out a little, putting a slight load on the engine.  When you push the clutch back in, it revs up some because the ECU had moved the idle air control pintle out a little in an attempt to keep the idle at the "set" rpm.  <P>On my 85 GT, the rpm doesn't go up when the a/c is on, there is a slight drop until the ECU can move the IAC pintle out to compensate for the extra load on the engine.||||reg||14
Z||000009||rockcrawl||10-28-2001||11:54 AM||||robertjay is correct, the idle is not supposed to increase when the A/C is turned on.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Dan010||10-28-2001||01:02 PM||||Good!!!!!!!!!!<BR>Thanks!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
